{"number": 111, "text": "            53)\n                          On     top     of   that...&4@;\n                          Adding         information              and      changing           topic\n\n\n                        - A number  of words  and phrases are used to introduce a piece of information that is related to\n                          what has just been said. Many  of these are typically said in their own speech unit with a rising\n                          or fall-rising tone:\n                                 A: You can get a €100  fine for dropping litter in the city centre.\n                                B: //on TOP   of THAT          the police can make  you spend a whole  day picking up\n                                   rubbish.\n                                A: Ill give you a ring later.\n                                 B: // BETter STILL      why  don’t I come over and  see you?\n                                A: We  haven’t got any coffee left.\n                                B: //in THAT   case           just have water, thanks.\n                                A: There’s no point in keeping this jacket, it’s full of holes.\n                                B: // ALso        the zip’s broken.\n\n                          Other words  and phrases  like this include: at the same 'time,\n                          in the same ‘way, ‘similarly, by the same ‘token, even ‘better,\n                          worse 'still, ‘otherwise, in other ‘words (or in ‘other words).\n                          (Note that the main stressed syllable is marked with '.)\n\n                     D40\n                         When   we  want to change  the topic in a conversation or to start talking about a different aspect\n                          of the same topic, we often begin with a word or phrase with a falling tone in its own speech\n                          unit:\n                                A: So where  did you say they lived?\n                                 B: Cornwall.\n                                A: Great, it’s really nice there.\n                                B: Yeah. // ANyway   6@//, | must dash. See you later.\n                                A: I really like the town square.\n                                B: Lovely, isn’t it? // RIGHT     so what  would  you like to see next?\n\n                          Other words  and phrases like this include:         by the 'way, inci'dentally (this suggests that\n                         what  is going to be said is less important than what has come before), 'now (then), o'kay, well.\n                          Some  people use /ook to introduce an aspect of the same topic that they particularly want the\n                          hearer(s) to pay attention to:\n                                 A: There’s a meeting this Friday afternoon.\n                                 B: // LOOK       I won’t be able to get there, so can you tell me what happens?\n                          However,  other people only use     this way to show  that they are annoyed:\n                                A: I don’t want to go to the dentist.\n                                 B: // LOOK       don’t be so childish!\n\n                         We   can use besides to give another reason or argument for something:\n                                 A: Maybe  we could  look around  the castle?\n                                 B: It’s really expensive to get in. // BESIDES  it’s only open in the morning.\n\n\n                112\n                             English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3541, "mean_conf": 90.85, "word_count": 426, "words": [{"text": "53)", "x0": 226, "y0": 91, "x1": 443, "y1": 263, "conf": 79, "bold": false}, {"text": "112", "x0": 312, "y0": 3190, "x1": 376, "y1": 3222, "conf": 95, "bold": true}, {"text": "39", "x0": 396, "y0": 342, "x1": 460, "y1": 376, "conf": 4, "bold": false}, {"text": "D40", "x0": 396, "y0": 1366, "x1": 462, 